proof.text:
Pass to underlying abelian sheaves. Both internal-to-section module equivalence and the module-sheaf forgetful node preserve these colimits. The underlying functors of the open extension and closed pushforward are respectively abelian extension by zero and abelian direct image. Open extension preserves colimits as a left adjoint. Each closed pushforward preserves filtered colimits by ringed open–closed localization, since its target is qcqs by step 1 and its open complement is qc by hypothesis. The pullback $v^*$ has underlying abelian inverse image by Cartesian coefficients, so also preserves colimits as a left adjoint. These descriptions are independent of which coefficient ring acts; they identify the transition maps with those induced by $I_F	o I_G$. Induction through the actual support chain therefore gives
$$ igl(v^*J_{R_Omega}(I_f) igr)_{
m ab}congarinjlim_{Fsupseteq E} igl(v^*J_{R_F}(I_F) igr)_{
m ab}.$$
The index is the small directed tail poset from the coefficient-colimit node, in the prescribed universe.

The affine test $U$ is qcqs. Apply bounded-below cohomology continuity on $X_{
m proet}$ to the last diagram of underlying abelian sheaves as complexes in degree zero, using the constant integer ring and common lower bound zero. It gives, for every $q$, the canonical isomorphism
$$H^q!left(U, igl(v^*J_{R_Omega}(I_f) igr)_{
m ab}
ight)congarinjlim_{Fsupseteq E}H^q!left(U, igl(v^*J_{R_F}(I_F) igr)_{
m ab}
ight).$$
Small ringed-site module categories have enough injectives: the K-injective existence node gives that they are Grothendieck, and the enough-injectives node applies. The module/abelian evaluation comparison, transported through the internal module equivalence, identifies these groups with exactly the module-derived section groups in the question. For $q>h$ every term on the right is zero by the assumed estimate, applied to $g_F$ on the same chain over the original $X$ and the same $v$. The colimit of zero groups is zero. This proves the corrected assertion with precisely the original bound $h$.

